n below the US, so the GDP-weighted OECD-38 central path is ~2.1-2.4% - BELOW the 2.50 bar. A regime where electricity, construction and care run 4-6% while software, media, professional services and tradable goods run 0-1% is exactly the configuration that leaves the CORE AGGREGATE unremarkable, because the hot items are either excluded from core (energy), weight-light, or publicly price-administered. MARGINAL A = 0.27 (20% base rate nudged up for fiscal deficits, the 17% health weight, and the financial-repression incentive at >100% debt).\nCONJUNCT B - China headline CPI <1.50%/yr average 2027-2036. Reference class: China NBS CPI 10-yr averages - 1995-2004 ~1.0%, 2000-09 ~2.0%, 2005-14 ~3.0%, 2010-19 ~2.4%, 2015-24 ~1.4%, 2020-25 ~0.8%, 2023-26 ~0.0-0.4%. Closest single reference class: post-property-bust aging East Asia. Japan's rolling 10-yr CPI averages were below 1.5% continuously from the window ending 2002 through the window ending ~2021 (1993-2002 ~0.3%, 1998-2007 ~-0.1%, 2003-12 ~-0.1%) - roughly two decades of consecutive hits. Taiwan 2000-2020 ~1.0%. Counter-cases: Korea post-1997 ran 2-3%. That reference class alone supports ~0.70-0.75. Discounts: AMRO (Jan 2024) concludes China's deflationary risks are \"rising but not as severe as Japan's case in 1990s\", and China's policy rate is materially higher than Japan's was, leaving easing room; headline (not core) is also food/pork/energy-levered over a 10-year window and an RMB devaluation is reflationary for headline. MARGINAL B = 0.58.\nCORRELATION: slightly NEGATIVE, rho ~ -0.20. Chinese deflation is exported into OECD core goods (23% of core PCE weight), which makes conjunct A harder; the 2014-2019 and 2023-2026 episodes of Chinese disinflation both coincided with OECD core undershooting target. So P(B|A) = 0.58 * 0.85 = 0.49.\nJOINT = 0.27 * 0.49 = 0.133 -> 14.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.263Z","seriesId":"published-panel","s